---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';

const SITE = 'https://lepidus-design-system-button.vercel.app';

const rulesBlock = `# UI rules — Lepidus Design System

This project uses Lepidus (${SITE}).

- Build UI with the documented lp- classes only. The complete inventory:
  ${SITE}/llms.txt
- Never hardcode colors, spacing, radii, or font sizes. Use
  var(--component-*) first, then var(--color-*) / var(--space-*) /
  var(--radius-*). Hardcoded hex values fail review.
- Theming: set data-theme="light" | "dark" | "hc" on <html>.
  Never write per-theme values by hand.
- Interactive components use lepidus.js: data-lp-tabs, data-lp-modal-open /
  data-lp-modal-close, data-lp-accordion, data-lp-stepper, data-lp-tooltip,
  or Lepidus.toast({...}) / Lepidus.openModal(id).
- Keep the documented accessibility markup: role="switch" + aria-checked on
  toggles, aria-invalid + aria-describedby on input errors, aria-current in
  breadcrumbs, aria-label on listboxes, role="img" + aria-label on avatars.`;

const mcpConfig = `{
  "mcpServers": {
    "lepidus": {
      "command": "node",
      "args": ["../lepidus-design-system/packages/mcp/server.mjs"]
    }
  }
}`;

const reviewGrep = `# any hex color outside a var() fallback is a violation
grep -rnE '#[0-9a-fA-F]{3,8}\\b' src/ --include='*.html' --include='*.css' --include='*.tsx'`;

const buildPrompt = `Build a "server details" settings page in plain HTML using Lepidus:
a name input, a scan-frequency radio group (hourly / daily / weekly), and a
danger "Delete server" button that opens a confirmation modal.
It must work under data-theme="dark" and "hc".`;
---

<Page
  title="AI & vibe coding"
  eyebrow="For AI assistants & their humans"
  lede="Make your assistant fluent in Lepidus in about five minutes. After setup, prompts like 'build the alerts page' come back with real lp- classes, live tokens, and the accessibility intact."
>
  <Callout variant="success" title="Everything on this page is tested">
    <p>The MCP tools, the <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a> rules, and the
    <a href="/component-manifest.json">component manifest</a> are exercised by this repo's own
    build — and the generated markup patterns pass axe (WCAG 2.1 AA) in CI across all three
    themes. If it's documented here, it runs.</p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 class="step"><span class="step__num" aria-hidden="true">1</span>Give your assistant the rules</h2>
  <p>
    Create one file in <strong>your product repo</strong> and paste the block below. This is the
    highest-impact two minutes of setup — assistants read it automatically and stop inventing
    class names:
  </p>
  <table class="doc-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>File to create</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Claude Code</td><td><code>CLAUDE.md</code> (repo root)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cursor</td><td><code>.cursorrules</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>GitHub Copilot</td><td><code>.github/copilot-instructions.md</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Anything else</td><td>Paste it into the system/context prompt</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="copy-wrap">
    <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy-pre>Copy</button>
    <Code code={rulesBlock} lang="markdown" theme="github-dark" />
  </div>

  <h2 class="step"><span class="step__num" aria-hidden="true">2</span>Wire the MCP server (recommended)</h2>
  <p>
    The rules tell the assistant <em>how to behave</em>; the MCP server lets it <em>look facts
    up mid-task</em> — exact props, the legal classes for a component, token values per theme.
    Add this to <code>.mcp.json</code> (Claude Code) or <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code> (Cursor)
    in your product repo:
  </p>
  <div class="copy-wrap">
    <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy-pre>Copy</button>
    <Code code={mcpConfig} lang="json" theme="github-dark" />
  </div>
  <p>
    Point <code>args</code> at your checkout of the design-system repo (the example assumes a
    sibling folder). Inside the design-system repo itself, <code>.mcp.json</code> already ships —
    nothing to do. The server is zero-dependency Node and read-only.
  </p>
  <table class="doc-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Ask it for</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>list_components</code></td><td>Every component, with plain-CSS availability</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>get_component</code></td><td>Props, lp- classes, and the import for one component</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>search_tokens</code></td><td>Token variables by name <em>or value</em> ("#1c4599" works), per theme</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>get_usage_rules</code></td><td>The full llms.txt contract</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 class="step"><span class="step__num" aria-hidden="true">3</span>Check it's working</h2>
  <p>Paste these into your assistant. Each has a verifiable right answer:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <em>"Which Lepidus components ship as plain CSS?"</em> — with MCP it calls
      <code>list_components</code>; you should see 27 components marked with their
      <code>lp-</code> block names (Button, Tag, Table, Modal…).
    </li>
    <li>
      <em>"What classes does the Tag component have?"</em> — expect the exact inventory:
      <code>lp-tag</code>, status modifiers, <code>lp-tag__label</code>,
      <code>lp-tag__remove</code>. If it invents anything else, the rules file isn't loaded.
    </li>
    <li>
      <em>"Which token holds the brand blue, and what is it in dark theme?"</em> — expect
      <code>--color-brand-primary</code> → <code>var(--blue-600)</code> in light,
      <code>var(--blue-400)</code> in dark, via <code>search_tokens</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    No MCP? The fallback works with any assistant that can fetch a URL:
    <em>"Read {SITE}/llms.txt and follow it for all UI in this project."</em>
  </p>

  <h2 class="step"><span class="step__num" aria-hidden="true">4</span>Prompts that work well</h2>
  <p>Be explicit about the stack and the theme contract — then ask for the screen, not the component:</p>
  <div class="copy-wrap">
    <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy-pre>Copy</button>
    <Code code={buildPrompt} lang="text" theme="github-dark" />
  </div>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Converting:</strong> <em>"Convert this page to @lepidus/react components, keeping every ARIA attribute."</em></li>
    <li><strong>Auditing:</strong> <em>"Audit this HTML against the Lepidus rules and list violations with fixes."</em></li>
    <li><strong>Seeding:</strong> for complex screens, paste the relevant Code-tab markup first — the assistant extends documented patterns instead of inventing structure.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="step"><span class="step__num" aria-hidden="true">5</span>Review the output like any other PR</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No raw hex values</strong> — one command finds every violation:</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="copy-wrap">
    <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-copy-pre>Copy</button>
    <Code code={reviewGrep} lang="bash" theme="github-dark" />
  </div>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Only documented classes</strong> — every <code>lp-*</code> class must appear in the
      component's <a href="/components/">Specifications tab</a> (the manifest is the complete legal set).</li>
    <li><strong>Accessibility survived</strong> — tab through it: focus rings visible, toggles are
      <code>role="switch"</code>, errors are announced, the modal traps focus.</li>
    <li><strong>Flip the theme</strong> — set <code>data-theme="dark"</code>; anything that breaks
      was hardcoded.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Under the hood</h2>
  <p>
    Three layers power all of this: <a href="/llms.txt"><code>/llms.txt</code></a> (the
    plain-language contract + class inventory), <a href="/component-manifest.json"><code>/component-manifest.json</code></a>
    (every prop and class in machine-readable form, regenerated on every build — the
    Specifications tabs on this site are this file, visualized), and the MCP server
    (<code>packages/mcp/server.mjs</code> — design doc in <code>packages/mcp/README.md</code>).
  </p>
</Page>

<script>
  document.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
    const btn = (e.target as HTMLElement).closest<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-copy-pre]');
    if (!btn) return;
    const pre = btn.parentElement?.querySelector('pre');
    if (!pre) return;
    navigator.clipboard
      .writeText(pre.textContent ?? '')
      .then(() => {
        btn.textContent = 'Copied ✓';
      })
      .catch(() => {
        // clipboard blocked (e.g. embedded preview) — select the text instead
        const range = document.createRange();
        range.selectNodeContents(pre);
        const sel = window.getSelection();
        sel?.removeAllRanges();
        sel?.addRange(range);
        btn.textContent = 'Press ⌘C';
      })
      .finally(() => setTimeout(() => (btn.textContent = 'Copy'), 1600));
  });
</script>
